lligence officers and former  military officers and Foreign Service  officers and so have now participated in  quite a number of the international  conferences on different aspects of the  Cold War and so forth and they were  prepared to sit down with Western former  counterparts if you wish or opposite  numbers and historians and others and  more or less some more and some less get  into the spirit of things and discuss it  what they are not what former Soviet  intelligence officers are not prepared  to do is to sit down in the same table  or the same conference with major  defectors the efforts have been made  and have always failed to get a former  retired KGB officers in good standing to  come to conferences with Gordievsky and  Kalugin and others and this they won't  do I can tell you one one thing from my  own experience of interviewing one  former KGB officer victor chu kashun who  is chief of counterintelligence in the  washington rezidentura in 1985 when all  of the year of the spy was going on he  was willing to talk to me a great length  of a few years ago about Aldrich Ames  who would by that time had already been  arrested and but what I didn't realize  at the time I was interviewing when I  thought he was being so expansive was  that he was also the man who had been  Robert Hansen's handler and I had no  idea at the time I was interviewing him  and I thought he was being so  cooperative that he had this other  secret that he was still keeping thank  you I think we're running out of time we  just a couple more minutes left last  question over here and then I apologize  there will be some time hopefully  afterwards to talk in person to the  co-authors and commentators the last  question  cliff Hackett Jean Monnet counsel I  don't know if this is better addressed  to a Raymond Garth Hoff or to Bearden  but if the PR B's attitude in recent  years has loosened up has become more  liberal does that mean that before that  liberalization that this book might h